and the final announcement in today's  budget takes a first step  madam deputy speaker  for many of the lowest paid in society  there is a hidden tax on work  the universal credit taper withdraw  support as people work more hours  the rate is currently 63  so for every one pound someone earns  their universal credit is reduced by 63p  let us be in no doubt this is a tax on  work  and a high rate of tax at that  organizations  organizations as varied as the trades  union congress the joseph rowntree  foundation the resolution foundation the  center for policy studies and the center  for social justice have all said it is  too high  so  to make sure  work pays  and helps some of the lowest income  families in our country keep more of  their hard-earned money  i have decided to cut this rate  not by one percent not by two percent  but by eight percent  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  this is a tax on working people and we  are cutting it from 63 to 55  the re the rate originally envisaged by  my right honorable friend the member for  chingford  and because i'm also increasing the work  allowances by 500 pounds this is a tax  cut next year worth over 2 billion  pounds nearly two million families will  keep on average an extra thousand pounds  a year  changes like this  normally take effect at the start of the  new tax year in april  but we want to help people right now so  we will introduce this within weeks and  no later than december the 1st  [Music]  [Applause]  let me tell the house what these changes  mean  a single mother of two renting and  working full time on the national living  wage will be better off by around 1 200  pounds  a couple renting a home with their two  children one working full-time the other  working part-time will be better off  every single year by 1 800 pounds  this is a 2 billion tax cut for the  lowest paid workers in our country it  supports working families it helps with  the cost of living and it rewards work  [Applause]
 

